title: 'pallet-revive: deny state changes from transfer and send receivers'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A contract called through Solidity's `transfer()` or `send()` gets a stipend of 2300 gas. On
    Ethereum, the stipend cannot pay for a storage write, a value transfer, or creating or
    terminating a contract. In revive, only the stipend's weight limited these operations.
    The pallet now limits the callee to what the stipend pays for on Ethereum, for every call with
    that shape: an EVM `CALL`, or a PolkaVM `call_evm` without `READ_ONLY`, that moves value with a
    gas limit of 0, or moves no value with a gas limit of exactly 2300. In the callee, and in every
    contract it calls, these operations fail with `OutOfGas`:
    - persistent contract storage writes (`SSTORE`, the PolkaVM storage host functions, and
      `clearStorage` and `takeStorage` of the storage precompile),
    - value transfers,
    - contract creation (`CREATE`, `CREATE2`, PolkaVM `instantiate`),
    - contract termination (`SELFDESTRUCT`, PolkaVM `terminate`, `terminate` of the system
      precompile),
    - calls to a state-changing function of the assets, asset-conversion, XCM or vesting
      precompile.

    The contract that attempts such an operation halts on the EVM or traps on PolkaVM, and its
    caller sees a failed call. On Ethereum, the operation runs out of gas, with the same result.
    When a precompile denies such an operation, only the call to the precompile fails. Reads, events
    and transient storage still work. Calls with any other explicit gas limit are not affected, even
    one below 2300.

    The receiver may call back into the sender, as on Ethereum, under the same limits. Before, the
    EVM denied this after a zero-value `transfer()` or `send()` and in the callee of a `STATICCALL`
    with a gas limit of 2300: the reentrant call returned 0 in an EVM receiver, and a PolkaVM
    receiver trapped.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `PrecompileExt::call` (exported as `pallet_revive::precompiles::Ext`) takes a `CallProtections`
    argument in place of its reentrancy protection and `read_only` arguments. The struct carries
    the reentrancy protection, `read_only`, and `stipend_limited`, which is set for calls shaped
    like a `transfer()` or `send()`. A precompile that passed `ReentrancyProtection::AllowReentry`
    and `false` now passes `CallProtections::none()`. Set any other reentrancy level on the
    `reentrancy` field, and a read-only call on the `read_only` field:
    ```rust
    // Before
    env.call(&resources, &to, value, input, ReentrancyProtection::Strict, true)?;
    // After
    let protections = CallProtections {
        reentrancy: ReentrancyProtection::Strict,
        read_only: true,
        stipend_limited: false,
    };
    env.call(&resources, &to, value, input, protections)?;
    ```

    `ReentrancyProtection::AllowNext` is removed and replaced by `stipend_limited`.

    In a stipend-limited frame, `PrecompileExt::set_storage`, `PrecompileExt::terminate_caller`,
    `PrecompileWithInfoExt::instantiate` and a value transfer through `PrecompileExt::call` return
    `OutOfGas`. In a read-only frame, they return `StateChangeDenied` instead, and so do
    `PrecompileExt::set_transient_storage` and a value transfer through `PrecompileExt::call` with
    `read_only` set. Before, only their callers checked read-only; now they check it themselves.
    A precompile that changes state by calling into another
    pallet, for example to move balances or assets, must call the new
    `PrecompileExt::ensure_not_stipend_limited` first. The assets, asset-conversion, XCM and
    vesting precompiles do so.

    `integrity_test` checks that a block cannot write more contract storage than a validator has
    memory for. It scaled a write's weight by the memory that write uses before dividing the block
    weight by it, so the storage term came out near zero and the check could not fail on it. It now
    computes how many writes fit the block weight before multiplying by the memory each one uses,
    and prices each write as a cold write without rollback.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-asset-conversion-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-xcm-precompiles
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-vesting-precompiles
  bump: patch
